<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lal Bahadur Shastri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1904">1904</a>–<a href="/years/1966">1966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lal Bahadur Shastri (1904–1966) was an Indian independence activist and Congress politician who succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> as prime minister in 1964. He led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the war of 1965 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and promoted food self-sufficiency with the slogan 'Jai Jawan, Jai Kisan' (Hail the soldier, hail the farmer). He died suddenly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> in January 1966, a day after signing a peace agreement with Pakistan.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lal Bahadur Shastri was born on 2 October 1904 at the home of his maternal grandparents in Mughalsarai. His family had no links to the independence movement, but a teacher at Harish Chandra High School, Nishkameshwar Prasad Mishra, inspired in him an interest in the freedom struggle. In January 1921, he attended a meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Benares</a> addressed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>. Answering Gandhi's call to students to leave government schools, he withdrew from school the next day and joined the Congress as a volunteer in the non-cooperation movement. He was jailed but released as a minor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1928 Shastri became an active member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s call and was imprisoned for two and a half years. After the Quit India speech of 8 August 1942 he directed activists for a week from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a>'s home, Anand Bhavan. In all he spent nearly nine years in British prisons. After independence he became Minister of Police and Transport in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> under Govind Ballabh Pant, and as Transport Minister was the first to appoint women conductors.</p><p>In 1951 he became General Secretary of the All-India Congress Committee and organised the party's successes in the general elections of 1952, 1957 and 1962. Nehru called him to the centre as Minister of Railways and Transport on 13 May 1952. After the Ariyalur train accident of 1956 he offered his resignation again, taking political and moral responsibility, and it was accepted; he left office on 7 December 1956. He later served as Minister of Commerce and Industry in 1959 and as Minister of Home Affairs in 1961.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Nehru died in office on 27 May 1964, and Congress president K. Kamaraj was instrumental in making Shastri prime minister on 9 June. In 1965 he created the National Dairy Development Board for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-revolution">White Revolution</a>, and he promoted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-revolution">Green Revolution</a>, which raised grain production in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>.</p><p>After skirmishes in the Kutch in August 1965 came the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, during which his slogan Jai Jawan, Jai Kisan became popular. He signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent-declaration">Tashkent Declaration</a> on 10 January 1966 and died the next day. Many of his supporters and relatives have refused to believe the circumstances of his death and have alleged foul play. He was awarded the Bharat Ratna posthumously, and the Vijay Ghat memorial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> commemorates him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lal Bahadur Shastri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Congress governed almost without interruption until 1977, under Nehru, <b>Lal Bahadur Shastri</b> and Indira Gandhi, who nationalised the banks in 1969.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Congress parliamentary party elected Indira Gandhi as its leader after the sudden death of <b>Lal Bahadur Shastri</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-19">19 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pandit Deen Dayal Upadhyaya Nagar, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.3,83.12&amp;year=1944" aria-label="Pandit Deen Dayal Upadhyaya Nagar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6752 1233 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lal Bahadur Shastri"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7202 1503h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7202 1503h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.3,83.12&amp;year=1944">What was here in 1944? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
